%I A401471 #13 Nov 04 2026 19:50:54
%S A401471 0,1,3,6,10,15,21,28,34,42,47,53
%N A401471 Minimum number of edges in a simple graph of treewidth n.
%C A401471 a(n) <= A000217(n), since the complete graph K_{n+1} has treewidth n and n(n+1)/2 edges. Equality holds for n <= 7, and n = 8 is the smallest n with a(n) < A000217(n).
%C A401471 a(n) is strictly increasing: deleting an edge lowers treewidth by at most 1, so deleting edges one at a time from a graph of treewidth n reaches treewidth n-1 with fewer edges.
%C A401471 a(n) grows linearly: the lower bound below is linear, and bounded-degree expanders have treewidth linear in their number of edges, so deleting edges from a suitable expander gives a graph of treewidth exactly n with O(n) edges.
%C A401471 Up to isomorphism, exactly two connected graphs with 34 edges have treewidth 8: a 4-regular graph on 17 vertices with graph6 code Ps`aa?aGOgAGBCA`@L?@K?ES, taken from the House of Graphs catalog of quartic graphs, and a graph on 16 vertices (twelve of degree 4 and four of degree 5) with graph6 code O?qbF_WF_cA__IOBCC_Af. Both are triangle-free. Every graph with 34 edges and treewidth 8 is one of these two plus isolated vertices.
%C A401471 a(9) = 42 is attained by a 4-regular graph of girth 5 on 21 vertices with graph6 code T???C@?GC_B_S_AICS_b_PEAcACS_BQ??F@?. Whether it is the only connected graph with 42 edges and treewidth 9 has not been determined.
%C A401471 a(10) = 47 is attained by a graph on 23 vertices with graph6 code Vs`AA?cGb?A_C_CA@_?S?AA?@`OAgOAT??aG?CW??G`_, obtained by adding one edge to a 4-regular graph of girth 5. Whether it is the only connected graph with 47 edges and treewidth 10 has not been determined.
%C A401471 a(11) = 53 is attained by a graph on 24 vertices, ten of degree 5 and fourteen of degree 4, with graph6 code W?dkC@?G?`AAO_GOOE?_FOP?aGG?c?@ACAC@?Q?gG@``?@w. Whether it is the only connected graph with 53 edges and treewidth 11 has not been determined.
%C A401471 The lower bounds a(7) >= 28, a(8) >= 34, a(9) >= 42, a(10) >= 47 and a(11) >= 53, and the classification at 34 edges, are computer-assisted. The Joret-Wood bound and vertex-elimination reductions show that every connected graph with at most 28 edges and treewidth at least 7 is K_8 or one particular 12-vertex graph, using an exhaustive enumeration of 96841893 graphs with nauty's geng; the bounds on a(7) and a(8) follow. The classification at 34 edges classifies 518071774 graphs with 29 edges and checks all 86221634 connected 4-regular graphs on 17 vertices. The lower bound a(9) >= 42 builds on the classifications at 28 and 34 edges; its largest step checks 22489604 extensions of 53898 classified 36-edge graphs. The lower bound a(10) >= 47 reduces to 4-regular graphs on 23 vertices: 969011 reconstructions from the classified 34- to 36-edge graphs when there is a 4-cycle, and the 3917 graphs of girth 5 otherwise. The lower bound a(11) >= 53 reduces to graphs with 52 edges, degrees 4 and 5, and 21 to 26 vertices, and excludes each number of vertices by a further case analysis. See the Davies link.
%H A401471 Antoine Amarilli, <a href="https://cstheory.stackexchange.com/questions/53806/">What is the smallest graph of treewidth k having less edges than the (k+1)-clique?</a>, Theoretical Computer Science Stack Exchange, 2024.
%H A401471 Jason Davies, <a href="https://github.com/jasondavies/treewidth-min-edges">Code, proofs, and certificates for a(0)-a(11)</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%H A401471 Jason Davies, <a href="https://cstheory.stackexchange.com/a/57305">Answer to the question above</a>, Theoretical Computer Science Stack Exchange, 2026.
%H A401471 House of Graphs, <a href="https://houseofgraphs.org/meta-directory/quartic">Quartic graphs</a>.
%H A401471 Gwenaël Joret and David R. Wood, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.04730">K4-minor-free induced subgraphs of sparse connected graphs</a>, arXiv:1605.04730 [math.CO], 2016; SIAM J. Discrete Math. 32 (2018), 123-147.
%F A401471 a(n) >= 16*(n-2)/3 - 1 for n >= 2, from the bound tw(G) <= 2 + floor(3*(m+1)/16) of Joret and Wood for a graph G with m edges.
%e A401471 a(2) = 3: a graph of treewidth 2 contains a cycle, and the triangle K_3 has 3 edges.
%e A401471 a(8) = 34: no graph with 33 or fewer edges has treewidth 8, and the 16-vertex and 17-vertex graphs above have 34 edges and treewidth 8, whereas K_9 has 36 edges.
%e A401471 a(9) = 42: no graph with 41 or fewer edges has treewidth 9, and the 21-vertex graph above has treewidth 9, whereas K_10 has 45 edges.
%e A401471 a(10) = 47: no graph with 46 or fewer edges has treewidth 10, and the 23-vertex graph above has treewidth 10, whereas K_11 has 55 edges.
%e A401471 a(11) = 53: no graph with 52 or fewer edges has treewidth 11, and the 24-vertex graph above has treewidth 11, whereas K_12 has 66 edges.
%Y A401471 Cf. A000217, A263294.
%Y A401471 Agrees with A124158 and A161208 for n <= 8.
%K A401471 nonn,hard,more,new
